Stop a structured-output request with invalid speculative drafts from taking the
engine down with `CUDA error: an illegal memory access was encountered`.

WHAT HAPPENS

`v1/structured_output/utils.py` reorders the grammar bitmask to match batch
order, then hands `out_indices` straight to a CUDA kernel:

    xgr.apply_token_bitmask_inplace(logits, grammar_bitmask, indices=index_tensor)

xgrammar's kernel indexes `logits` with those values and does not bounds-check
them. The indices are built as:

    num_spec_tokens = len(spec_tokens.get(req_id, ()))
    for i in range(1 + num_spec_tokens):
        out_indices.append(logit_idx + i)

`num_spec_tokens` counts the speculative *slots the scheduler reserved*, not the
drafts that turned out valid. vLLM's invalid-draft sentinel is -1 (see
`is_valid_mask = (token_ids != -1) & ...` in v1/spec_decode/utils.py:219). When
a request's drafts come back invalid, `logit_idx + num_spec_tokens` can point
past the end of `logits`, and the kernel reads out of bounds.

The fault is asynchronous, so it surfaces at whatever unrelated CUDA sync point
comes next -- in the observed case `synchronize_input_prep` inside a dynamo
compile of `_prepare_inputs`, which makes the traceback actively misleading.

OBSERVED (2026-08-26 07:23:19, this box)

Hermes' chat-title generator -- a small text-only request that ends "Reply with
JSON only", i.e. guided decoding -- scheduled with three invalid drafts while
sharing a batch with an 832-token prefill chunk:

    num_scheduled_tokens={a57b4806...: 4, a71be0b5...: 832}
    scheduled_spec_decode_tokens={a57b4806...: [-1, -1, -1]}
    has_structured_output_requests=true
    pending_structured_output_tokens=true

`apply_token_bitmask_inplace_kernel` JIT-compiled during inference in the same
second (jit_monitor warning), consistent with a shape it had never seen.

This is very likely the same fault as the "**Known rare crash, not yours to
fix**: `torch.AcceleratorError: CUDA error: an illegal memory access` under
3-way load (seen once, 2026-08-23)" recorded in HIT_DIVERGED_FIX_PLAN.md:163 --
which predates all vision work, so this is NOT a vision-tower regression.
ENCOURAGING_WORDS.md implicates `--async-scheduling` in that crash;
`--async-scheduling` is on by default in 0.27.1 and active in this deployment
(`ASYNC_ARGS`, single-user/start_qwen.sh:187). Whether async scheduling widens
the window for a mismatch between reserved spec slots and actual logits rows is
untested. Note the 2026-08-23 traceback was never saved and its log has since
been rotated away, so "same fault" is inference from a matching signature, not
a confirmed match.

THE FIX

Bounds-check each bitmask row index against `logits.shape[0]` (and the source
row against `grammar_bitmask.shape[0]`) before writing it or appending it to
`out_indices`. Dropping an out-of-range row leaves that one position
un-grammar-masked for a single step, which the next step corrects; indexing it
takes the whole engine down. That trade is not close.

`cumulative_index` still advances by the full reserved amount, because that is
the stride the structured-output manager used when laying out the bitmask's
rows -- changing it would misalign every later request in the batch.

A `logger.warning` fires whenever any row is skipped, reporting the row counts,
the request ids and their spec tokens, so it is visible whether this ever
actually triggers rather than silently papering over the condition. If it never
fires, the diagnosis above is wrong and this patch is inert -- which is the
point of logging it.

Grep for: `structured-output bitmask: skipped`

